 "rules": [
  {
   "id": "safety",
   "file": "safety.md",
   "title": "Strict Safety & System Constraints",
   "body": "## 1. Absolute Negative Constraints\n- **NEVER** run destructive Git operations (`git reset --hard`, `git clean -fd`, `git push --force`, or unverified branch deletion).\n- **NEVER** execute destructive system commands (`rm -rf`, `find -delete`, or mass file truncations).\n- **NEVER** modify root system configurations or install global packages (`sudo`, `pacman -S`, `aur`, `cargo install --global`) without explicit user command.\n- **NEVER** read, output, or expose secret files (`.env`, private keys, GPG/SSH keys, token files).\n\n## 2. Command Execution Safety\n- **State Modifications:** Any command that creates, updates, or deletes files or directories must be explicitly confirmed.\n- **Background Processes:** Do not start persistent background processes or system daemons without prior permission.\n- **Failure Halting:** If a command returns a non-zero exit code or fails a build check, **STOP immediately**. State the error clearly and wait for instructions. Do not attempt automated fix loops.\n\n## 3. Environment Protection\n- Operate strictly within the current workspace root directory.\n- Respect `.gitignore` rules and local configuration locks.\n"
  },
  {
   "id": "workflow",
   "file": "workflow.md",
   "title": "Development Workflow & Execution Standards",
   "body": "## 1. Code Modification Protocol\n- **Minimal Touch:** Implement only the specific change requested. Do not attempt unrequested refactoring, code formatting re-ordering, or cosmetic modifications.\n- **Plan Presentation:** For multi-file changes or structural edits, provide a brief execution outline before requesting permission to apply edits.\n- **API Integrity:** Preserve function signatures, type contracts, and public interfaces unless an explicit breaking change is requested.\n\n## 2. Quality Gates & Verification\n- **Verification Strategy:** State how the proposed change will be tested or verified prior to editing.\n- **Targeted Testing:** Prefer focused, targeted test executions over full suite runs unless specifically requested.\n\n## 3. Commit & Signature Standards\n- When creating Git commit messages, apply the standard developer credit line format:\n  `Project contributor`\n- Match commit messages to conventional standards (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`) matching the exact intent of the edit.\n"
  },
  {
   "id": "concise-output",
   "file": "concise-output.md",
   "title": "Concise, Action-First Output",
   "body": "## 1. Lead With the Answer\n- **First line answers:** Put the result, decision, or answer first. Supporting detail follows only if it helps.\n- **No preamble:** Do not restate the question, announce what you are about to do at length, or thank the user for asking.\n- **No filler:** Cut hedging chains, praise, and generic advice that does not change what the user does next.\n\n## 2. Match Length to Need\n- **Small question, small answer:** A one-line question gets a short reply. Expand only when the task needs it.\n- **Requested depth wins:** When the user asks for detail, a report, a plan, or an explanation, give the full depth asked for.\n- **Show changes, not files:** When reporting code changes, show the relevant lines or summarise the diff instead of pasting whole files.\n- **One status line:** During long tasks, give brief progress updates (what you are doing now), not running commentary.\n\n## 3. Make It Scannable\n- **Structure helps:** Use short paragraphs, bullet lists, and tables when they make content faster to scan; plain sentences when they do not.\n- **Plain words:** Prefer simple, specific wording over jargon. Define a term once if it is unavoidable.\n- **Concrete over abstract:** Give the exact command, path, error message, or number rather than a description of it.\n\n## 4. Never Trade These for Brevity\n- **Safety and risk:** Warnings, stop-and-report notices, and irreversible-action confirmations are always stated in full.\n- **Accuracy:** Do not drop caveats that change the meaning (failed tests, skipped steps, assumptions, uncertainty).\n- **Evidence:** When a skill or task requires a report, findings, or verification output, include it completely.\n"
  },
  {
   "id": "rust",
   "file": "rust.md",
   "title": "Rust Development Standards",
   "body": "## 1. Idiomatic Code & Safety\n- **Avoid Unsafe Code:** Never use `unsafe` blocks without explicit user approval and a detailed safety comment explaining invariants.\n- **Error Handling:** Avoid `.unwrap()` and `.expect()` in production library code; return `Result<T, E>` and utilize proper error propagation (`?`).\n- **Memory Management:** Minimize unnecessary allocations and prefer borrowing (`&T`, `&mut T`) over cloning (`.clone()`) where lifetime constraints allow.\n\n## 2. Toolchain & Testing\n- Use standard cargo commands (`cargo check`, `cargo test`, `cargo clippy`).\n- Ensure all compiler warnings are resolved before confirming code changes.\n"
  },
  {
   "id": "gleam",
   "file": "gleam.md",
   "title": "Gleam Development Standards",
   "body": "## 1. Type Safety & Architecture\n- **Type Annotations:** Leverage Gleam's strong static type system; let type inference work where appropriate, but explicitly type public module boundaries.\n- **Error Handling:** Utilize the `Result(data, error)` type rather than throwing exceptions or panics.\n- **BEAM Ecosystem:** Keep modules aligned with Erlang/OTP idioms when interacting with actors or supervision trees.\n"
  }
 ]
